  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and thunderstorms Tuesday afternoon and \n   evening, with gusty outflow winds of 30-50 mph, moderate to \n   heavy rainfall, and lightning, strongest storms south of \n   Highway 200.\n\n-  Another round of storms Wednesday capable of 30-50 mph outflows\n   and heavy rain rates up to 0.5 inches per hour.\n\n-  Persistent active pattern late week into the weekend under\n   southwesterly flow, with a 15% chance of a tropical moisture\n   tap on Sunday.\n\nToday through Tuesday Night:\nA ridge of high pressure will be over the region today, resulting\nin a warming and drying trend with minimal wind across the\nNorthern Rockies. High temperatures will continue to rebound \nfollowing chilly morning conditions. \n\nConditions shift by Tuesday afternoon as an atmospheric\ndisturbance pushes northward out of Lemhi county and southern\nIdaho county into western Montana. This feature will trigger\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms across the area. The\nstrongest convective activity is anticipated south of Highway 200\nin western Montana, where storms will be capable of producing\ngusty outflow winds of 30 to 50 mph, lightning, and brief moderate\nto heavy rainfall. Storm activity will move out of the forecast\narea by midnight Tuesday night.\n\nWednesday through Weekend:\nA stronger disturbance will traverse the Northern Rockies\nWednesday morning through the afternoon. Storms on Wednesday will\nonce again pose a threat for gusty outflow winds reaching 30 to \n50 mph. Heavy rain potential appears enhanced with this system, \nwith rainfall rates up to 0.5 inches per hour possible under \nstronger cores. Extreme southwest Montana will also be dealing \nwith gusty winds and low afternoon humidity Wednesday afternoon \nand evening potentially creating critical fire weather conditions.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will persist Thursday into Friday as an\nupper-level trough of low pressure settles over the Pacific\nNorthwest. This setup will keep temperatures near to slightly\nbelow average for late summer, along with periodic showers and\nthunderstorms, particularly during pressure trough over the region\nthrough the weekend. Roughly 15% of ensemble guidance indicates\nthe potential for a tap of significant tropical moisture on\nSunday. Regardless of weather this moisture plume materializes, \nthe Northern Rockies will remain embedded in an active, unsettled \nweather pattern through the weekend.",
